{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Arise, Balak, etc.</b> Why this rising? And our Sages of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 20:20) said that because Balak was mocking Balaam, that he is not master of himself, therefore he intended to provoke him and said to him: Arise and hear. And according to their words, one needs to know why he said ״וּשְׁמַע״ with the addition of the letter vav. And perhaps he intended to say to him that he should begin to hear from the moment of rising, and this is his saying <b>Arise and hear.</b>" "Or it may be that, since HaShem said to Balaam ״Return to Balak״ etc., obligating him to say the matters before Balak — therefore, if he had said ״Arise, Balak, hear,״ it would be understood that his intent was that he should arise to hear so that he might hear standing; but there would be no obligation regarding the hearing — if he did not wish to hear he could turn and go on his way. Therefore he said ״and hear״ — an obligation of rising and an obligation of hearing." "<b>Further,</b> he intended to say to him that his rising is for his own merit, that he has earned to hear matters coming from a supernal place. And he further aroused him to incline his ear toward him, to hearken to all the precise nuances of the matters. And in this you are greater in rank than the one whom you regard as a father — who is the bird of the sorcerers, as it is written ״בֶּן צִפּוֹר״; from now on he is your son, and this is his saying ״בְּנוֹ צִפּוֹר״." "<b>Further,</b> the verse may be explicated according to what we have introduced, that this wicked man had in him two evil traits: the first, that he wished to curse Israel, and an angel needed to come to bend shut his mouth as stated; and the second, that even the blessing itself that issued from his mouth he intended for disparagement, and the holy spirit needed to interpret it for praise, as we have written above. And since Balak suspected Balaam that he desired to bless Israel, at the beginning of his words he said to Balak that he should stand upon his standing to scrutinize two details, so that he would not suspect him. And this is the first, corresponding to what he would say in his blessing matters that are not so explicitly for good and the holy spirit would interpret them as complete blessing — and this is his saying <b>and hear</b> with the addition of the letter vav, to allude to another hearing. And corresponding to what he wished to curse and the angel was bending shut his mouth, he said <b>hearken unto me</b> — meaning, that he should draw closer toward him and perceive by the bending shut of his lips, for they become bent shut from the utterance that he would utter with his lips. And by this he aroused him that he was under duress, and they would not suspect him of blessing his enemies."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {2 "The comment plays on Balak's title shifting from בן צפור (Numbers 22:2 etc.) to בנו צפור (Numbers 23:19, 24:14), reading the latter as 'his son' rather than the standard 'son of Zippor.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:21.710783739Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}